Create the virtual environment:cd backend
python -m venv venv 

Activate the environment:
venv\Scripts\activate
 
install FastAPI, the server web server (Uvicorn), database drivers, and the necessary AI/NLP data libraries:
pip install fastapi uvicorn mysql-connector-python spacy scikit-learn pydantic python-multipart


Download the NLP Model inside the environment:
python -m spacy download en_core_web_sm

Open frontend folder in another cmd:
For frontend library :  
npm create vite@latest . -- --template react

Install frontend dependencies locally:
npm install

Install Plotly & Router packages:
These will power your interactive dashboard charts and page routing locally:
npm install react-router-dom plotly.js-basic-dist react-plotly.js lucide-react


career-intelligence-platform/
├── backend/
│   ├── venv/             <-- Isolated Python environments & packages
│   └── (Your future python code files will go here)
└── frontend/
    ├── node_modules/     <-- Isolated Javascript libraries
    ├── src/
    ├── package.json
    └── index.html


for frontend: inside frontend folder
mkdir src\components src\pages src\context src\mockData

src/
├── components/   <-- Reusable UI parts like Navbar, Sidebar, Layout wrappers
├── context/      <-- Global application state (simulating a login session)
├── mockData/     <-- Hardcoded JSON files representing database responses
├── pages/        <-- The actual pages matching your UI design image
├── App.jsx
└── main.jsx

Create the Global Mock Data StoreTo make your frontend feel interactive and realistic, we will create a mock data file. 
It provides fallback values for charts, skills, and roadmaps.
Create a new file named src/mockData/careerData.js in VS Code


Establish the Global Shared Shell Layout (Sidebar Navigation)According to views 4 through 15 in your UI sheet, your app features a consistent left-hand Sidebar Navigation Menu. Let's build that structural component.Create a file named src/components/Sidebar.jsx

Configure the Routing Registry EngineLet's assemble the dynamic core inside src/App.jsx to tie everything together. For this step, we will use mock placeholders for your main dashboard pages so that routing works immediately.

Verify the ArchitectureLet's ensure your layout framework behaves correctly. 
In your VS Code Frontend Terminal Tab, run your dev script:   npm run dev


Create the Dashboard Page ComponentCreate a new file named src/pages/Dashboard.jsx

Create the Resume Analysis PageCreate a new file named src/pages/ResumeAnalysis.jsx

Since you are building out the core AI decision-support logic, our next perfect target is View 9 (Skill Gap Analysis) coupled with View 12 (The What-If Career Simulation Panel).By grouping these two together on one clean page, your users can review their current skill deficiencies and instantly simulate how adding new skills shifts their alignment.
Create the Skill Gap & Simulator Page ComponentCreate a new file named src/pages/GapAnalysis.jsx in VS Code


Create View 11 — The Career Roadmap PageThis page generates a structural, step-by-step timeline using nodes. Each node represents a skill gap, and clicking on it dynamically displays external tutorial resources (Requirements 14 & 16).Create a new file named src/pages/Roadmap.jsx

Create View 13 — The Job Market Intelligence PageThis dashboard uses Plotly charts to analyze live job market patterns, tracking which skills are in high demand across the industry (Requirement 17).Create a new file named src/pages/MarketIntelligence.jsx
Vite stores a compilation map inside your node_modules directory that can sometimes lock up. Let's run a clean boot by executing this exact command in the VS Code CMD Terminal:cmdnpm run dev -- --force
Use code with caution.(The -- --force flag commands Vite to completely delete its internal build caches and rebuild the workspace freshly from scratch).


